{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is broadly accurate: Indigenous peoples have resisted governments that imposed colonization, dispossession, forced assimilation, or restrictions on their autonomy. Examples include Māori resistance to British colonial expansion, Native American resistance to removal policies, and Aboriginal activism against discriminatory Australian laws.\n\nHowever, “Indigenous peoples” encompasses many distinct societies, and “oppressive governments” needs context. Resistance has included armed conflict, legal challenges, political organizing, and cultural preservation. The claim is strongest when tied to specific peoples, governments, policies, and periods."}}
